#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Fermi-Hubbard model describes electrons hopping on a lattice with an "
"energy penalty for double occupancy. On a one-dimensional chain of $L$ sites"
" with open boundaries, the Hamiltonian is defined as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"H = -\\sum_{i=0}^{L-2} \\sum_{\\sigma \\in \\{\\uparrow,\\downarrow\\}} t_{h,i} \\left( c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma} c_{i+1,\\sigma} + \\mathrm{h.c.} \\right) + \\sum_{i=0}^{L-1} U_i\\, n_{i\\uparrow} n_{i\\downarrow} - \\sum_{i=0}^{L-1} \\sum_\\sigma \\mu_i\\, n_{i\\sigma},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma}$ creates a fermion of spin $\\sigma$ on site "
"$i$ and $n_{i\\sigma} = c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma} c_{i\\sigma}$ counts them. The"
" three terms are the hopping amplitude $t_{h,i}$ on the bond between sites "
"$i$ and $i+1$, the on-site interaction $U_i$, and the chemical potential "
"$\\mu_i$. The tutorial measures energy in units of $t_h$ and time in units "
"of $1/t_h$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Real-time dynamics of this model are hard to compute classically. Exact "
"diagonalization stops near $L \\lesssim 14$ sites, and after a quench, the "
"entanglement entropy of a tensor-network solver grows linearly in time, so "
"the bond dimension needed for fixed accuracy grows exponentially.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In one dimension, the elementary excitations are spinons, which carry spin "
"and no charge, and holons, which carry charge and no spin. The two propagate"
" at different speeds, so a localized disturbance splits into a fast front "
"and a slow one. This effect is called spin-charge separation, and this "
"tutorial measures it [\\[1\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses the Dynamics Simulator Qiskit Function, which takes the "
"Hamiltonian, an initial state, a Trotter schedule, and a list of "
"observables, and returns expectation values. It handles the Jordan-Wigner "
"mapping, Trotterization, qubit layout, and error suppression internally, so "
"there are no circuits to build or transpile in this tutorial.\n"
msgstr ""

