#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-"
"inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Define the Hamiltonian {#define-the-hamiltonian}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The function takes the model as a dictionary with three keys. Each one "
"accepts either a single float, which applies uniformly along the chain, or a"
" list that specifies the value site by site. Lists have $L-1$ entries for "
"`hopping_amplitude`, because an open chain has one fewer bond than sites, "
"and $L$ entries for `on_site_interaction` and `chemical_potential`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses a uniform chain of 31 sites with a repulsive interaction "
"$U/t_h = 8$. A uniform chemical potential contributes only a global phase "
"when the particle number is fixed, so this example sets it to zero. The "
"chemical potential matters only when it varies from site to site, for "
"example, to add disorder or a single impurity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Set the Trotter schedule {#set-the-trotter-schedule}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `simulation` dictionary holds the time step ($\\Delta t$) and the number"
" of Trotter steps, and the total evolution time is their product. Larger "
"steps reach later times at fixed circuit depth but incur more Trotter error,"
" which grows with $U$ because the Hamiltonian terms commute less. The "
"published hardware study used $\\Delta t = 0.15$ for $U/t_h = 8$ "
"[\\[2\\]](#references), and this tutorial uses the same value. Circuit depth"
" grows linearly in the step count and does not depend on $L$ at all.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prepare the initial state {#prepare-the-initial-state}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each site holds two spin orbitals, so an $L$-site chain uses $2L$ qubits. "
"The initial state is a Fock occupation string with one character per site:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\texttt{empty (e)} &\\longleftrightarrow \\left(n_{i,\\uparrow}, n_{i,\\downarrow}\\right) = (0,0), \\\\\n"
"\\texttt{up (u)} &\\longleftrightarrow \\left(n_{i,\\uparrow}, n_{i,\\downarrow}\\right) = (1,0), \\\\\n"
"\\texttt{down (d)} &\\longleftrightarrow \\left(n_{i,\\uparrow}, n_{i,\\downarrow}\\right) = (0,1), \\\\\n"
"\\texttt{both (b)} &\\longleftrightarrow \\left(n_{i,\\uparrow}, n_{i,\\downarrow}\\right) = (1,1).\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The string is read least significant site first, so the rightmost character "
"is site 0 and the physical site index of character $k$ counted from the left"
" is $i = L - 1 - k$. The string `udud` therefore means site 0 is `d`, site 1"
" is `u`, site 2 is `d`, and site 3 is `u`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, prepare a half-filled Néel state, alternating `u` and `d`, with the "
"central site replaced by a vacancy. The vacancy is the disturbance whose "
"spreading is tracked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build the observables {#build-the-observables}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each observable is a dictionary that represents a weighted sum of products "
"of occupation operators:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"O = \\sum_{\\alpha} c_{\\alpha} \\prod_{(\\sigma, j) \\in T_{\\alpha}} n_{j,\\sigma}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every entry of `operators` is one term $T_{\\alpha}$, written as a list of "
"`[name, site]` pairs that multiply together, and the matching entry of "
"`coefficients` is its weight. The two names are `\"Nup\"` for "
"$n_{j,\\uparrow}$ and `\"Ndn\"` for $n_{j,\\downarrow}$. A single one-point "
"observable $n_{3,\\uparrow}$ is written as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The next cell prints the smallest complete set of inputs the function "
"accepts, which is useful as a shape reference while reading the code that "
"follows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Three groups of observables are needed. The charge density $n_i = "
"n_{i\\uparrow} + n_{i\\downarrow}$ and the local spin $S^z_i = "
"(n_{i\\uparrow} - n_{i\\downarrow})/2$ are both two-term sums over the same "
"operators, differing only in their coefficients. The spin correlator $S^z_i "
"S^z_{i_*}$ between site $i$ and the vacancy site expands into four two-point"
" terms with coefficients $\\pm 1/4$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Occupation operators commute, so the order of factors within a term is "
"irrelevant, but the order of the `observables` list fixes the order of the "
"returned values. The code records an index for each group while it builds "
"the list. The correlator skips the vacancy site, where the product would "
"repeat a factor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-"
"problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial has no transpilation step. The function performs the following"
" steps internally [\\[2\\]](#references), and you cannot configure them from"
" the notebook:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Maps the fermionic modes to qubits under an ordering chosen to keep Pauli "
"weights low on a heavy-hex lattice\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Uses a fermionic SWAP network so every hopping term becomes a two-qubit "
"rotation\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Embeds the chain along a path that avoids weak qubits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Applies dynamical decoupling and Pauli twirling during compilation\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You choose the backend and the shot count. The function takes a backend name"
" as a string, so this tutorial selects the least busy device large enough "
"for the chain and passes its name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The smallest signal that you need to resolve sets the shot count. The charge"
" tracer reaches amplitudes near $0.15$, but the connected spin tracer peaks "
"around $0.04$, roughly four times smaller. The published study used 20,000 "
"shots per circuit for this reason [\\[2\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute by using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-by-using-qiskit-"
"primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next, submit the simulation. `run` returns a job handle immediately rather "
"than blocking.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use `job.status()` to follow progress and `job.result()` to block until the "
"job finishes. The result is a mapping whose `observable_expectation_values` "
"entry is indexed first by observable, in the order they were supplied, and "
"then by time. There are `step_count + 1` time points because the value at "
"$t=0$ is included.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reconstruct the correlators {#reconstruct-the-correlators}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Split the flat array back into the three groups using the indices recorded "
"earlier, then form the two tracer correlators. The charge tracer is the "
"change in per-site density relative to $t=0$:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"C^c_i(t) = \\langle n_{i\\uparrow}(t) + n_{i\\downarrow}(t) \\rangle - \\langle n_{i\\uparrow}(0) + n_{i\\downarrow}(0) \\rangle.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The spin tracer is the connected correlator between each site and the "
"vacancy site $i_*$:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"C^s_i(t) = 4 \\left( \\langle S^z_i S^z_{i_*} \\rangle - \\langle S^z_i \\rangle \\langle S^z_{i_*} \\rangle \\right).\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both are built from Fock-basis operators, so they come from the same "
"measured bitstrings and the second one costs no extra hardware execution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When you constructed the observables, the code recorded the position of each"
" charge (`charge_indices`), spin (`spin_indices`), and spin-correlation "
"(`correlator_indices`) operator in the list. Because the simulator returns "
"the expectation values in the same order, you can use these positions to "
"extract the corresponding results. For the spin correlations, the code also "
"uses `correlator_sites` to place each result at the correct lattice "
"position, and leaves the vacancy as `NaN`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Visualize the wavefronts {#visualize-the-wavefronts}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If you plot each correlator as a heatmap of space and time, the light cone "
"is directly visible. Time flows downward, and the horizontal axis is the "
"position along the chain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/fermi-hubbard-"
"spin-charge-dynamics/extracted-outputs/c75549b7-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Extract the wavefront velocities {#extract-the-wavefront-velocities}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Turn the heatmaps into numbers by following the detection protocol from the "
"published study [\\[2\\]](#references). At each time slice, symmetrize the "
"spatial profile about the vacancy, which averages away the antisymmetric "
"part of the hardware noise, normalize by the largest off-center amplitude, "
"and record the outermost site above a threshold. Detections are then cut "
"into runs of consecutive time steps, and only the longest run is fit, which "
"keeps the early-time transient out of the result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The fitted velocity depends on the detection threshold and the trimming "
"quantile, and that sensitivity dominates the shot noise. Scan both and "
"report the median with the full envelope, as the published analysis does.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/fermi-hubbard-"
"spin-charge-dynamics/extracted-outputs/640db359-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Drawing the detections back onto the heatmaps shows whether the algorithm tracked\n"
"the leading edge. Filled markers are the detections that entered\n"
"the fit. Open markers were cut by the masking rules, either because they sit too\n"
"close to the vacancy, too close to the chain end, or were late enough that the front has\n"
"broadened and its position is no longer sharp. The dashed line is the fit over the\n"
"times it used, and its slope is the velocity in each panel title.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The vacancy column is marked below the axis. The charge clips there because its value\n"
"is of order one, and spin has no value there, so it is drawn in gray.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The charge cone opens wider than the spin cone. Both disturbances leave the same\n"
"defect at the same moment and travel outward at different speeds.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/fermi-hubbard-"
"spin-charge-dynamics/extracted-outputs/25192e92-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The charge front outruns the spin front. A hole in a one-dimensional Mott "
"background does not travel as an electron. Instead, it fractionalizes into a"
" holon carrying the charge and a spinon carrying the spin disturbance, and "
"at $U/t_h = 8$ the spinon is the slower of the two.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The ordering depends on the interaction. At $U = 0$, the model is free "
"fermions with $v_c = 2$ and $v_s = 4$, so spin is faster. Repulsion "
"suppresses spin transport while leaving charge transport nearly untouched, "
"and by $U/t_h = 8$ the published hardware values are $v_c = 1.78$ and $v_s ="
" 1.46$ [\\[2\\]](#references). Rerunning with several values of $U$ and "
"plotting $v_s / v_c$ maps the crossover.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note these caveats on the numbers. Threshold-based detection is biased "
"slightly low, so a charge velocity a few percent under the published value "
"is expected. The spin envelope is wide because the connected correlator sits"
" closer to the noise floor, which is why the median of the scan rather than "
"a single fit is quoted.\n"
msgstr ""

